docs/ux-design-specification.md:42:The first challenge is designing for two reading distances within one composition. In 2-3 seconds from across the foyer, users must understand whether the departure picture is broadly calm, strained, or disrupted, and whether the screen appears trustworthy. At closer range, the same screen must reward inspection with factual clarity about nearby modes, weather, and local relevance.
docs/ux-design-specification.md:56:A third opportunity is to use visual hierarchy as the core UX differentiator. A strong atmospheric first read, a fixed local map, fact-only mode comparison, and selective trust/trend cues can create a distinctive design language that feels calm, local, and specific to the Royal Institution.
docs/ux-design-specification.md:92:The second critical success moment happens at closer range, when the user confirms the overall picture through factual detail about nearby modes, weather, trust cues, and local relevance just before leaving. The far read creates orientation; the near read confirms action.
